WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.028
Agents answer when you ask.

2
00:00:03.028 --> 00:00:07.971
Automations act on their own — on
schedules, on events, on incoming work.

3
00:00:07.971 --> 00:00:11.425
This episode: how they
run, how they fail honestly,

4
00:00:11.425 --> 00:00:14.337
and the card that
keeps a person in charge.

5
00:00:15.937 --> 00:00:19.159
The catalog is a library
of ready-made bundles:

6
00:00:19.159 --> 00:00:23.409
sync a mailbox, resolve GitHub
issues, keep documents indexed.

7
00:00:23.409 --> 00:00:27.385
Each one packs workflows,
agents, and views — one install,

8
00:00:27.385 --> 00:00:30.127
and it files itself into your workspace.

9
00:00:31.327 --> 00:00:34.691
This workspace already
runs one: task triage.

10
00:00:34.691 --> 00:00:40.597
Every new task gets scored, and the
automation decides — assign it to an agent,

11
00:00:40.597 --> 00:00:43.139
or leave a suggestion for a human.

12
00:00:43.139 --> 00:00:44.485
Let us open it up.

13
00:00:45.685 --> 00:00:50.358
Inside, the automation reads like
a recipe: a trigger, then steps.

14
00:00:50.358 --> 00:00:55.385
A step scores the task with an
agent, a step routes it, a step reports.

15
00:00:55.385 --> 00:01:00.555
No black box — you can read exactly
what will happen before it ever runs.

16
00:01:01.755 --> 00:01:03.707
Every run leaves a journal.

17
00:01:03.707 --> 00:01:07.899
Green rows completed; each
one lists what the trigger saw,

18
00:01:07.899 --> 00:01:10.284
what each step did, what it cost.

19
00:01:10.284 --> 00:01:14.115
Automation you can audit is
automation you can trust.

20
00:01:15.315 --> 00:01:18.710
And here is the row that
matters most: a red one.

21
00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.758
A step returned data
that failed validation,

22
00:01:21.758 --> 00:01:25.361
the run stopped, and the
journal says exactly where.

23
00:01:25.361 --> 00:01:31.111
Automations fail sometimes — the
difference between a toy and a tool is whether the

24
00:01:31.111 --> 00:01:32.705
failure has an address.

25
00:01:33.905 --> 00:01:35.558
Now the centerpiece.

26
00:01:35.558 --> 00:01:39.442
We ask for a customer
reply — drafted AND sent.

27
00:01:39.442 --> 00:01:42.582
The agent writes the draft… and stops.

28
00:01:42.582 --> 00:01:46.383
This card is the
boundary: the draft is ready,

29
00:01:46.383 --> 00:01:50.184
but touching the
outside world needs a person.

30
00:01:50.184 --> 00:01:52.995
We read it, we decide, we approve.

31
00:01:59.905 --> 00:02:05.488
AI drafts, people decide — episode one
made the promise, this is the wiring.

32
00:02:05.488 --> 00:02:10.777
And notice what approvals really buy
you: not less automation, but more.

33
00:02:10.777 --> 00:02:16.948
You can afford to automate the risky work
precisely because the risky step waits for

34
00:02:16.948 --> 00:02:17.242
you.

35
00:02:18.442 --> 00:02:21.957
Automations: readable
recipes, honest journals,

36
00:02:21.957 --> 00:02:25.098
and a human gate on the steps that matter.

37
00:02:25.098 --> 00:02:30.482
Next episode: projects — where your
team and its agents share one board.

38
00:02:31.682 --> 00:02:36.440
The automations and approvals
sections of the documentation carry the full

39
00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:37.140
reference.

40
00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.562
See you in episode six.

